const faq = [
  { q: 'How do ravens find carrion?', a: 'Ravens use a combination of vision and social information to locate carrion. They soar high to scan large areas — the visual acuity of corvids at height allows detection of potential carrion sites from considerable distance. Social information is equally important: ravens watch other ravens, following birds that suddenly descend or exhibit excited behaviour. At communal roosts, ravens that found food on a given day can be followed back to the food source the next morning — Bernd Heinrich\'s information-centre hypothesis.' },
  { q: 'Can ravens smell carrion?', a: 'The Common Raven\'s olfactory capability is not well-established compared to vultures (which have exceptional olfaction). Ravens are primarily visual hunters and foragers. They likely detect very strong odours from large decomposing carcasses at close range, but olfaction is probably not the primary carrion-detection mechanism as it is in New World vultures. The evidence base for raven olfaction in carrion detection is limited — it has not been systematically tested.' },
  { q: 'Do ravens share carrion with others?', a: 'The pattern is complex. At large carcasses, non-breeding flocks can number dozens or hundreds of birds — apparently shared access. Research by Bernd Heinrich documented that young ravens actively recruit flock-mates to large carcasses on territory-holding adults\' land — a strategy that overwhelms the territorial pair with numbers, allowing the flocking birds collectively to exploit the food. Dominant birds preferentially feed; subordinates get access when dominants are satiated or displaced. Pure sharing is rare — carcass use is contested.' },
];
